WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:02.190
SPEAKER 1: OK, now for our
final speaker of the day.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.720
We were chatting after lunch.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.540
We think it was three or maybe
four years ago that she last

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.170
spoke for us.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.210
And we're glad she
could come back.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.810
Sharyl Attkisson is a
five-time Emmy award winning

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.820
investigative journalist.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.820
Many of you know her as the
managing editor and host

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.120
of Sinclair Broadcast Group's
excellent Sunday Morning

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.670
News program "Full Measure."

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.760
Previously, she was a
correspondent for "CBS News"

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.340
and an anchor and
correspondent for CNN.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.610
And I promised her we wouldn't
hold that last bit about CNN

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.410
against her.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.590
In addition, she hosted "Health
Week," a weekly medical news

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.690
magazine on PBS.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.870
In addition to her Emmys,
her many other awards,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.190
include The Barbara Olson Award
for Excellence and Independence

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.000
in Journalism, and the Edward
R Murrow Award for Excellence

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.690
in Investigative Reporting.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.780
Somehow in the midst of her
top-notch reporting work,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.410
she finds time to write
best selling books.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.110
The most recent of these
is titled, "Slanted:

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.800
How the News Media Taught Us
to Love Censorship and Hate

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.910
Journalism."

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.930
She'll be drawing on themes from
that book in her talk today.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.986
Would you please welcome back
to the podium, Sharyl Attkisson.

00:01:16.986 --> 00:01:18.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.230
SHARYL ATTKISSON: So good
to be here in person.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.950
I haven't been doing many
in-person appearances.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.570
Boy, can you guys see me?

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.790
I feel like I'm totally hidden.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:41.940
Yeah.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.280
If you guys have a stool, if
someone has a small thing,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.405
maybe you want to bring it.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:49.370
OK.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:54.940
I, too, have some slides that
I can't see at all from here.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.150
Maybe I'll stand over here.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.095
Now I'm messing up
the camera shot.

00:02:00.095 --> 00:02:01.970
Well, I'll just stand
on my toes a little bit

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.880
until we get a stool.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:09.910
But today I'm speaking about,
let's see the first slide,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.460
journalism and
the 2020 election.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.220
Slanted journalism.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.087
And I hope you can
take today's speech,

00:02:18.087 --> 00:02:19.420
maybe have a little fun with it.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.070
Because as sad as
these trends are, maybe

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.160
when we talk about them
together, and think about ways

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.490
to stay informed and
address these things,

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.820
we can at least be entertained
by some of the outrage,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.860
really, that the news
business has become.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.720
Let's begin by tracing the
well-organized and well-funded

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.792
movement to corner the market
on information and narratives.

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:49.680
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.560
This movement, I think, was
designed specifically in part,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.860
to keep President Trump
from serving his full term,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.780
or if that failed, to
impact the 2020 race.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.870
This whole strategy relied
heavily on slanted journalism.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.410
And that required
major news outlets

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.530
to suspend their normal
ethics and guidelines,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.190
which astonishingly enough,
they did, unabashedly.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.170
And admitted to it,
outright, saying

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.750
that it was necessary to
change the way we do business

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.990
because President Trump
was so uniquely dangerous.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.140
So in the past few years,
we've experienced a sea change

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.890
in terms of how the
media do their job.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.440
The emergence of Trump as a
viable political candidate

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.690
accelerated this devolution.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.170
No longer do reporters, as
you know, keep their opinions

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.820
firewalled from their stories.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.580
Their stories are rife
with their opinions.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.650
Dubious anonymous
sources are repeatedly

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.650
relied upon even after
they've repeatedly

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.590
proven shamefully unreliable.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:05.030
Basic fact checks go unconducted
as long as the news furthers

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.490
an anti-Trump narrative.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.980
Egregious reporting mistakes,
and we'll talk about some

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.610
of those today, are made by
the same outlets, and sometimes

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.730
the same reporters,
over and over again.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:19.730
And yet are magically forgiven.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:21.829
Journalism ethical
standards are bent

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.680
or suspended so that Trump could
be covered more aggressively

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.240
and in a one-sided fashion.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.380
Some of the best
evidence for this

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.500
can be found in a June
2017 opinion piece

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.723
by a guy named Mitchell
Stephens and it

00:04:36.723 --> 00:04:37.890
was published in "Politico."

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.050
Stephens is a
journalism professor

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.210
at New York University.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:47.130
In his article, he cheers on
the end of media objectivity.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.810
Oh, hold on just a moment.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.640
We may have a solution here.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.140
Thank you.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.130
That ought to do it.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:04:59.910
Thank you.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:00.540
Much better.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:01.780
Thank you.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:02.280
OK.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.720
I'll be careful.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.080
Thank you.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.630
So he cheers on the end
of media objectivity

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:12.090
under a new President Trump, who
was under attack by the media.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:16.010
The headline to his
article was, "Goodbye

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.480
Nonpartisan Journalism.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:18.920
And good riddance.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:22.630
Disinterested reporting
is overrated."

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.810
It's shocking that a journalism
professor, someone teaching

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.570
up-and-coming professionals
in the field of news,

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.380
considers disinterested
reporting to be overrated.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.450
Disinterested reporting
was, I thought,

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.145
always thought of as a
pillar of good journalism.

00:05:37.145 --> 00:05:39.520
Tossing it aside is, I think,
a bit like a medical school

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.240
professor telling medical school
students that diet and exercise

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.800
are overrated.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.050
In fact, they're fundamental.

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.500
Neutrality and objectivity in
news reporting are fundamental.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.900
But if one were trying
to brainwash people

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.180
into thinking
journalism is really

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.730
about the opposite
thing, then yes, one

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:02.890
would say the fundamentals are
outdated and to be rejected.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:05.620
Stephens goes on in
his very partisan piece

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.770
to heap praise upon the New
York Times' public dissent

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.780
into blatant partisanship.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.470
He writes, "Our most
respected mainstream

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.150
journalism organizations
are beginning

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.390
to recognize the failings
of nonpartisanship.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:24.850
Its tepidness, its blindspots,
its omissions, its evasions."

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.400
He says, "It was news
when the patriarch

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.650
of American journalism,
The New York Times,

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:34.300
finally used the word 'lie' in
a headline atop its front page

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:39.610
September 17, 2016, to
describe a Trump assertion."

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.910
Stephens goes on to note with
satisfaction that other legacy

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.530
media followed suit.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.560
He wrote, "Other legacy
journalism organizations

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.440
began more regularly calling
out Trump's falsehoods

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.980
if not actually
accusing him of lying."

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.530
About a week later,
the Los Angeles Times

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.310
declared, also on
page one, "Never

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:04.160
in modern presidential
politics has a major candidate

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.570
made false statements as
routinely as Trump has."

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.480
On April 5th, 2017,
The New York Times,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.150
reflecting the New
World Order quickly

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.120
changed a headline
from, "Trump says

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.450
Susan Rice may have
committed a crime," to,

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:24.300
"Trump, citing no
evidence, suggests

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.450
Susan Rice committed a crime."

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.570
Anchorman Scott Pelley
at CBS upped the ante

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.260
by calling Trump's statements
"divorced from reality."

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.080
No attribution, just Pelley's
own critical opinion.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.970
So what the Journalism
Professor Stephens

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.230
sees as worthy of a claim,
I see as irresponsible.

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:46.570
A journalism professor is
teaching a new generation

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:49.750
of reporters to inject
agendas and opinions

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.600
in their reporting, to
forget about the firewall

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.680
that we used to attempt to
put between news and opinion.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.162
That kind of reporting
that Stephens applauds

00:07:59.162 --> 00:08:01.120
would have gotten a
traditional news journalist

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.940
fired not all that long ago.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.730
Today it's part of what makes
it so easy for the narrative

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.640
to take hold and can make it
so difficult for the truth

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.860
to be told.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:13.540
There's endless
evidence for this sort

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.005
of slanted reporting today.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:20.320
Little quiz for you.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.970
Based on this headline
in The Atlantic,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:29.330
who would you guess came out
on top in a September 2019

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:30.530
election in North Carolina?

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:31.572
Democrats or Republicans?

00:08:31.572 --> 00:08:37.100
It says, "North Carolina Gives
Republicans a Wake-Up Call.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:38.539
The results of a
special election

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:42.409
portend trouble for
the GOP in 2020."

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:44.090
Sure sounds as
though Republicans

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.362
got their clocks cleaned.

00:08:45.362 --> 00:08:46.820
After all, the
headline states they

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.770
got a wake-up call
and the results

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.510
portend trouble for the GOP.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.240
So you might be
surprised to learn,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.790
as I was that, the
Republicans actually

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.800
had a successful night.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.480
It's laid out right
there in the article

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.147
if you get past the
misleading headline.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.910
Here we go.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.760
In a congressional race,
the Republican candidate

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.660
beat the Democrat by a
far wider margin, it says,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.280
of 4,000 votes.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.920
And separately, in a
state special election,

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.280
Republican Greg Murphy
won as expected.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.710
How on Earth did those
Republican victories

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:29.970
elicit a headline implying
that the Republican had lost?

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.760
It's as if somebody was bent on
pushing a particular narrative,

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:37.530
regardless of how the
actual election turned out.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.248
In this way, we can
see how reporters

00:09:39.248 --> 00:09:41.040
who are pushing a
narrative don't care much

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.420
about the facts.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.280
Pesky facts that
contradict a narrative

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.410
are nothing more than a
nuisance to brush off.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.140
Reporters simply devise
ways to dispense of them.

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:55.470
Another example demonstrates
nearly every trademark

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:56.970
of a narrative and
the perils that

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.370
come with reporters
advancing it blindly.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:04.230
On April 15, 2020, Politico
reports that President Trump

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.510
owed the Bank of
China tens of millions

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:11.580
of in a loan coming due in
2022 as he dealt with China

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.400
on the coronavirus pandemic.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.990
The implication
there is that Trump

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.440
can't be as tough as he
needs to be on China,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.090
and Chinese leaders,
for unleashing

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.330
COVID-19 and covering up
its seriousness because he

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.370
is beholden to them.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.090
This news makes headlines
around the world.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.550
"Trump owes tens of millions
to the Bank of China.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.100
And the loan is
due soon," blares

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.900
the headline on Politico.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.700
"Donald Trump's debt
to China," reads

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.890
The New York Times' headline.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:44.010
And National Review,
"Trump owes millions

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.730
to Bank of China for
building loan, reports show."

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.710
But it isn't true.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:52.500
Shortly after this news circled
the Earth, the Bank of China

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:56.400
issued a statement saying it
had held that Trump loan only

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:02.970
for 22 days before selling it to
a US real estate firm in 2012.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:04.860
In other words, tens
of millions of dollars

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.750
that Trump supposedly
owed China,

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.890
due soon, was actually owed
for only three weeks back

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.910
in 2012, eight years before.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.020
Obviously, these facts
negate the whole idea

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:17.770
behind this story.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.330
But the media are not about to
admit that they made a mistake.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.430
Politico simply
changes its headline,

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.760
Trump owes, to Trump owed China.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.320
Past tense.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.570
They and the other media
pretend the false information

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.750
they published had little
bearing on the actual news.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.940
National Review,
likewise, simply changes

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.270
Trump owes to Trump owed.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.370
Politico could have
avoided the error

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.550
if it hadn't failed to
follow a basic rule that's

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.550
taught to every 19- and
20-year-old journalism student.

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.710
Contact the people you're
talking about in a story

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:50.502
before you publish.

00:11:54.132 --> 00:11:57.780
Of the three reporters bylined
in the article, Marc Caputo,

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.660
Meridith McGraw and Anita
Kumar, none apparently

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:02.340
thought to contact
the Bank of China

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.780
before publishing the story.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.730
And apparently,
no editor thought

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.100
it was necessary
to do so, either.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.973
Had they done their
job, they likely

00:12:09.973 --> 00:12:11.640
would have learned,
prior to publication

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.230
as they were supposed
to, that there

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:16.560
was no current loan to Trump
from China, thus avoiding

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.400
their major flub.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:21.620
There are so many
of these mistakes.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.210
it starts to look
like a pattern,

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.580
a strategy against Trump.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.350
Not just sloppy mistakes.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.600
And I tracked the
major media mistakes

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.690
and never found one
that favored Trump.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.630
That starts to look
like a pattern, too.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:38.510
On Thanksgiving Day,
2019, a little less

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.460
than a year from
election day, we

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.460
passed the one hundredth
major media mistake

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.530
in the era of
Trump, by my count.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:52.440
This error was committed by
Newsweek's political reporter,

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.670
Jessica Kwong, who wrote a story
and published a tweet asking,

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:58.530
"How did Trump
spend Thanksgiving?

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.867
Tweeting, golfing, and more."

00:13:00.867 --> 00:13:02.700
She went on to write
that Trump was spending

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.280
Thanksgiving Day at
his Mar-a-Lago resort

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:06.870
in Palm Beach, Florida.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.750
The story implied that Trump
was, once again, goofing off

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.970
compared to his heroic
predecessor, President

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:14.760
Obama, who used to only
do selfless things.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:19.430
The problem is, her
story was false.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.650
Trump had actually left
Florida the evening

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:24.560
before Thanksgiving to
fly to Afghanistan, where

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:26.840
he spent the
holiday, not golfing,

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:30.053
but serving dinner to US troops.

00:13:30.053 --> 00:13:31.496
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.830
When her mistake became
clear the reporter, Kwong,

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:40.735
claimed she'd made
an honest mistake.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.570
On Twitter, she wrote that
she was deleting her earlier,

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:49.510
incorrect tweet with fabricated
information about Trump

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:52.900
because, quote, "It was
written before knowing

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:57.430
about the president's surprise
visit to Afghanistan."

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:00.190
There are five key problems
with this explanation

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:02.010
from an ethical
standpoint in journalism.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:03.260
So I'm going to go over those.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:06.670
First, she demonstrated
a shocking lack

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:08.520
of reportorial knowledge.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.602
I'm not a political
reporter, nor do I closely

00:14:10.602 --> 00:14:11.560
follow the White House.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.800
Yet, I knew enough to
wonder, whether on a holiday,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:17.680
the president might be making
a surprise visit to the troops

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:20.620
on Thanksgiving because all
recent presidents have done

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.600
this at one time or another.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:24.670
One would think a national
political reporter

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:27.940
and her editors would know to
watch for a possible surprise

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.690
visit by the president.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:32.880
Second, that Newsweek
article demonstrated

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.933
an inexcusable
failure to attribute.

00:14:35.933 --> 00:14:37.350
Kwong's mistake
wouldn't have been

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:41.070
as problematic if she had
attributed the claim that Trump

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.380
was golfing on Thanksgiving
to an actual source,

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.390
instead of reporting
it as if it were

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:49.050
her own firsthand
confirmed information.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:50.550
Kwong isn't the
only one who seems

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.860
to have abandoned the
basic journalistic practice

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.860
of attributing
information to its source.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.500
Reporters now routinely
declare information

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.380
to be a fact as if they have
personally confirmed it,

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.140
when they could not
possibly have done so.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.420
There was no election
fraud, or there

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.342
wasn't enough to change the
outcome of the election.

00:15:09.342 --> 00:15:10.800
The reporters
claiming these things

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.585
haven't gone to any precincts
and examined records

00:15:13.585 --> 00:15:15.210
or interviewed
witnesses on the ground.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:19.500
They're just declaring something
they want to believe as a fact.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:21.330
Third, the problem with
the Newsweek story,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.480
there was a baffling
failure to fact check.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.240
This is one of the most
basic tenets of journalism.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:29.460
No matter how obvious
something seems,

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:31.650
no matter how many
others are reporting it,

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:33.750
no matter how
obvious a video clip

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:37.862
may seem to show something,
it often proves to be wrong.

00:15:37.862 --> 00:15:39.570
That's why it's so
critical for reporters

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.190
to check their assumptions.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.050
Kwong should have
contacted the White House

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.660
to see if her claim, that
Trump would be golfing

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:47.940
on Thanksgiving, was true.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.980
If the people
there had said yes,

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:52.200
and she attributed the
answer to the White House,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.977
then even if Trump had
ended up in Afghanistan,

00:15:54.977 --> 00:15:57.060
Kwong could not have been
journalistically faulted

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.380
for the mistaken information.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:01.110
She would have done
her job correctly.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.240
Instead, she reported
false information

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.190
as if she had checked it.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:10.450
Fourth, there was
a glaring failure

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.460
to correct the mistake
after the fact.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:17.080
Although Newsweek fixed the
story, it really correct it.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.810
Editors called the
revision an update.

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:20.920
This is disingenuous.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:23.800
The story hadn't
changed or been updated.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:27.160
Newsweek simply learned that
its original report was false.

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:31.190
That merits both a
correction and an apology.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:34.210
And fifth, the false
information persisted well

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:35.920
after the update.

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:37.870
Newsweek retained
its false headline

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.180
stating that Trump
golfed on Thanksgiving.

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:41.290
He didn't.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:42.790
I think that's still
up there today,

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:44.560
because I checked
in the last week.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:57.100
I really think one of the
most remarkable things

00:16:57.100 --> 00:17:00.610
about this whole story, is
that it's a case of history

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:02.790
repeating itself.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:06.460
It's an example of lessons
not learned by the media.

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:09.940
NBC had generated a similar
scandal less than a year

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.150
before, also tying
itself up into a pretzel

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:17.589
to make it seem as if it
had not bungled a story.

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:22.000
The NBC saga began
on December 25, 2018,

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.630
nearly eight hours before
Christmas Day's official end.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:26.619
The timing matters.

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:28.630
You'll see why in a moment.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:31.090
The network published
a headline blaring,

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:34.600
"Trump becomes the first
president since 2002

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:37.870
not to visit troops
at Christmastime."

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.910
The story claimed
that Trump had broken

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:42.700
from a recent tradition
of actually visiting

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.460
troops and wounded warriors.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.290
The article took
multiple jabs at Trump

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:50.170
to advance the anti-Trump
narrative of a president who

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.300
could not live up to the
standards of his predecessors.

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:56.620
What NBC did not know, was
that contrary to its reporting,

00:17:56.620 --> 00:18:02.300
Trump had left the White
House late on December 25

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:03.485
to visit the troops in Iraq.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:14.600
What happened when the mistake
was revealed is quite telling.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:18.230
Like Newsweek, NBC was
unable to admit its mistake.

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:20.840
Instead of a simple
apology saying

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:23.300
it originally had no idea
the president had sneaked off

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.270
to Iraq and that it had made
an assumption without bothering

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:32.070
to verify it, NBC published
a lengthy editor's note

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:35.520
parsing the definition of what
constitutes a Christmastime

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:39.450
visit and claimed
the original article

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:41.040
was technically correct.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.620
Depends on what the meaning
of the word "is" is.

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:45.120
OK.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:49.230
So the NBC editor makes the
argument, well, maybe Trump

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.050
visited the troops around
Christmas, after all.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:54.180
And yes, he did leave the
White House on Christmas

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:55.530
to go to Iraq.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:58.290
But he did not arrive in
Iraq until after the stroke

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:00.000
of midnight.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.100
And the day after
Christmas isn't really

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.810
a Christmastime visit, is it?

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:08.100
So instead of an apology,
NBC defends its mistake

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:13.030
by arguing when Christmastime
technically begins and ends.

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:16.710
The NBC's editor's note reads--

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:21.300
this is so twisted, "As of
the end of Christmas Day 2018,

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.360
Trump had not visited the troops
during the holiday season,

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:26.680
and had announced
no plans to do so.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:28.560
The article was correct.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.710
But on December 26,
the situation changed.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:33.870
Trump and the First
Lady, Melania Trump,

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.810
made an unannounced
visit to troops in Iraq.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.670
As a result, the
thrust of this article

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:42.030
is no longer correct, even
if it was at the time.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.620
In the interest of
transparency, we

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:46.470
are keeping the
article on NBCNews.com

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:48.630
so that the record will
reflect the situation

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.390
on the day the
article was published,

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:54.300
and are directing readers to
an article about Trump's Iraq

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:56.010
visit here.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:59.370
We're also altering one line
in the article, as well as

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.470
the headline, to
be more specific

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:05.760
and to note Trump was the first
president since 2002 who didn't

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:09.270
visit military personnel
on or before Christmas,

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:10.920
rather than at Christmastime."

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:16.560
Claiming the article was correct
at the time but the situation

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.680
changed, without acknowledging
the journalistic malpractice

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:21.610
at issue, here, is remarkable.

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.670
The corrected
article then goes on

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:27.090
to double down on its mistake,
in a way that's still designed

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:28.710
to make Trump look bad.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:31.290
Because, after all,
that's the goal.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:34.020
First, NBC chooses
a new tack that

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:35.670
fits the narrative
it set out to prove

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.610
and stuck by it,
regardless of the facts.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:41.550
"By staying home on
Tuesday," said NBC,

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.520
"Trump became the first
president since 2002

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.620
who didn't visit
military personnel

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.030
on or before Christmas."

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:53.370
And then elaborates, "Based
on a check of NBC logs,

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:56.970
President Barack Obama visited
troops at Marine Corps Base

00:20:56.970 --> 00:21:01.496
Hawaii every Christmas he
was in office, [LAUGHTER]

00:21:01.496 --> 00:21:05.230
from 2009 to 2016.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.270
Before him," said
NBC, "according

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:10.540
to a check of news releases,
President George W Bush

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:16.180
visited wounded warriors at
Walter Reed from 2003 to 2008.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:19.360
Trump previously said,"
NBC goes on to say,

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:21.850
"he hasn't visited a combat
zone because he's had

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:25.480
an unbelievably busy schedule."

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:30.540
Among the anti-Trump press,
the new version of the story,

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:33.990
comparing Trump unfavorably
to Obama and Bush, goes viral.

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:36.510
No attention is paid
to NBC'S mistake.

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:38.310
It's like it never
even happened.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:40.620
The focus becomes the
newly revised scandal

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.690
that Trump is the
first president not

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:46.410
to visit the troops on
or before Christmas Day.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:49.920
One exception to the
groupthink, which surprised me,

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:51.960
is the normally,
anti-Trump, Erik

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.880
Wemple of the Washington Post.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:57.330
He actually took issue
with NBC pointing out,

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:00.060
"Trump didn't stay
home Christmas Tuesday,

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.880
as NBC claimed in the updated
version of its article,

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:04.560
at least not all of Tuesday.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:08.730
He left the White House
late on December 25."

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:12.150
Wemple goes on to say,
"the NBC story appears

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:16.740
to rest on a lawyerly definition
of Christmastime, Christmas

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.290
Day, and the days
and weeks before it.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:22.050
However, other common
definitions of Christmastime

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:25.320
expand that understanding
to the Christmas season,

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:29.100
or the period from about
December 24 to January 1,

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.240
or January 6.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:33.600
But," Wemple says,
"NBC seemed intent

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.480
on making the story be about
whether they could technically

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:38.790
defend that they
hadn't jumped the gun

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.567
and made a false assumption,
rather than what Trump actually

00:22:41.567 --> 00:22:42.900
did over the Christmas holiday."

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:48.440
NBC could have
chosen numerous facts

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:50.000
to highlight about
President Trump,

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.490
if it were focused on
disseminating information

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:54.960
rather than generating a smear.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:57.350
So let's look at some of
the actual stats I found

00:22:57.350 --> 00:22:59.930
when I did a little research.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.880
It turns out
President Obama never

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.630
visited US troops in foreign
countries or combat zones

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:08.060
on Thanksgiving or
at Christmastime,

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:11.420
as Trump had done
twice by early 2020.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:14.480
The closest President Obama
got to spending Thanksgiving

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:16.940
or Christmas in a combat
zone with the troops,

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:19.220
was a visit to
South Korea a week

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:22.610
before Thanksgiving in 2009.

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:24.440
President Bill
Clinton was kind of

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:30.080
close to a Christmas visit
with a December 22, 2007, visit

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:30.740
to Bosnia.

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.320
But that was after
the war ended.

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:35.180
The annual Christmas
troops visit

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:38.690
that NBC rushed to credit
Obama for, all of those

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.840
had taken place not
in a combat zone,

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:46.460
but stateside, conveniently
near Obama's home in Hawaii.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:48.290
Not that there's
anything wrong with that.

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:49.790
But what I'm saying
is, if they were

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:51.890
looking to disseminate
factual information,

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.980
they could have come up with
an entirely different story.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:59.030
Another point made in
the slanted NBC article

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:04.250
about Trump, was that he had yet
to visit an active combat zone,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:07.040
as if it were an anomaly,
that he supposedly

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:10.460
had gone almost two years
without having done, so.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:12.710
In fact, according
to military records,

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:16.280
presidential visits to combat
zones are relatively rare.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:18.230
Presidents have
visited a combat zone

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:22.640
only 27 times in US
history, prior to Trump.

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:26.420
By early 2020, Trump
had added at least four

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:29.180
to that historic tally.

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:32.750
Visits to South Korea,
Iraq, Afghanistan,

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:34.250
not to mention that
he was sometimes

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:37.160
accompanied by First Lady,
Melania, who has made at least

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.140
two visits to a combat zone.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:45.750
I discovered another
interesting point

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.970
while researching presidential
trips to combat zones.

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:50.400
Although you might
not immediately

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.890
think of South Korea
as a combat zone,

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:55.650
it technically is one, because
our war with North Korea

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:57.900
was never officially
declared over.

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:00.150
Both North Korea
and the US, which

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.630
is allied with South Korea,
patrol a demilitarized zone

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:05.070
that divides the two Koreas.

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:13.290
But remarkably, when counting
Obama's combat zone visits,

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:17.460
the military and media included
his trips to South Korea

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:20.790
as notches on his belt. But
they did not count visits

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:23.280
to the same place for Trump.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:25.980
At the very time when the press
was claiming Trump had never

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:32.040
visited a combat zone, he had
made two trips to South Korea.

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:40.960
Punctuating the
point, Wikipedia--

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:42.700
oops, can I go backwards?

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:50.810
Punctuating the point,
Trump's Wikipedia page

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:53.270
claims that Trump's
first combat zone

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:57.560
visit was on December
26, 2018, to Iraq.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:01.490
It, too, ignored his earlier
visit to South Korea.

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:05.510
In contrast, the official tally
for Obama counts all three

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:09.410
of his trips to South Korea
as combat zone visits.

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:11.480
Apparently, among
the slanted media,

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:14.450
South Korea is a combat
zone when Obama visits,

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:17.820
but not when Trump does.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:21.110
So here are seven other true
headlines that could have been

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:23.810
written about Trump's
Christmastime visit to Iraq,

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:25.460
but weren't.

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:29.060
Trump Becomes the First
US President in 16 Years

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:32.360
to Visit US Troops
in a Combat Zone.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.460
Trump is One of Only
Three US Presidents

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:38.720
to Spend Thanksgiving Day with
US Troops in a Combat Zone.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.750
Melania Trump Becomes
the First First Lady

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:45.710
to Spend Christmastime Visiting
US troops in a War Zone.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:48.380
Trump is First President
Since George W Bush

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:52.310
to Spend Thanksgiving with
Troops in a Foreign Country.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.770
President Trump is Only
the Third US President,

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:57.290
All of Them Republicans,
to Spend Thanksgiving

00:26:57.290 --> 00:27:02.000
with US Troops in a Combat
Zone or US Troops Anywhere.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.550
Trump is the Only President to
Visit the Troops in a Combat

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:06.680
Zone So Close to Christmas Day.

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:10.670
And President Trump is the
Only US President in History

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:14.270
to Visit US Troops Both in a
Combat Zone on Thanksgiving

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:16.252
and So Close to Christmas.

00:27:16.252 --> 00:27:17.698
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:17.698 --> 00:27:19.626
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:31.280
So this deep dive into just
a couple of specific cases

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:34.160
demonstrates, I think,
quite clearly how

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:36.710
the media has changed
the way it operates.

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:38.690
Largely, as I described
in my last book,

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:42.860
The Smear, because the media
has been successfully influenced

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:45.500
and in fact infiltrated
by corporate and political

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:48.410
interests.

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:51.530
This explains why
it's no mystery

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:55.530
and no accident, the devolution
of the news, as I call it,

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:57.350
is by design.

00:27:57.350 --> 00:28:00.710
And it ties in quite directly to
this accelerated and dangerous

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:03.510
trend towards censorship we've
seen the past four years.

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:08.160
But in particular, I'd
say the last six months.

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:13.980
I'll summarize by saying, on
the censorship trend, in 2016,

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:16.410
by that time the news
landscape, in my view,

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:18.660
had largely been dominated
by political and corporate

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:21.420
interests, who were defining
the terms of what we could

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.640
and couldn't report about, the
language that we had to use,

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:27.240
and the kind of people
we could interview.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.770
But these people saw that Trump
got elected anyway in 2016,

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:33.450
despite the fact that
most of the media

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:35.090
had told people
not to vote for him

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:37.410
and that he was
inherently dangerous.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:40.680
So these people, seeking to
influence the information

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.740
landscape, they
blame the internet.

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:47.160
They thought that people were
largely controlled in the news

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:48.420
that they could get.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:51.060
But we could still go online
and get other opinions,

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:55.000
see other scientific studies,
hear other viewpoints.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:58.890
So they set about, I think,
spending the last four

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:02.260
years trying to figure out
how to control the internet.

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:04.380
And if you remember,
before 2016,

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:06.960
Big Tech did not
have great interest

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.190
in this sort of censorship.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:11.340
There were issues with
privacy and other problems.

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:15.210
But they were not doing what
I call fake fact checks.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:19.080
They were not censoring accounts
and interfering between us

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:24.010
and our information to tell us
what we can and cannot believe.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:27.610
Now fact checks, whether
they're done by nonprofits

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:30.895
or newsrooms, are largely
funded and influenced

00:29:30.895 --> 00:29:32.770
by political and corporate
interests who want

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:34.640
to control that information.

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:39.070
And in fact, David
Brock of Media Matters,

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:42.790
the propaganda group, took
credit for convincing Facebook

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:47.140
right after the 2016 election,
to start up those fake fact

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:47.860
checks.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.820
This was a lobbying campaign.

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:55.200
Fact checks now become simply
another tool of propagandists.

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:56.610
How do we fight censorship?

00:29:56.610 --> 00:30:00.570
Well, when a person or idea
is fact checked, or banned,

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:03.030
or censored, or
controversialized,

00:30:03.030 --> 00:30:04.590
I think we should
make it our mission

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:07.320
to find out more about
whatever is being censored.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:10.110
And ask the question,
why do powerful interests

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:12.090
not want me to have
this information,

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:15.340
and make up my own mind?

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:17.940
OK, our final examination
of how the media changed

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:22.080
the way it operated to impact
the 2020 election and control

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:27.230
narratives, looks at the
verbiage of the narrative.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:29.600
Before Trump, we
as news reporters,

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:32.600
might have pointed out
discrepancies or contradictions

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:36.162
between claims made by
a news maker, and facts.

00:30:36.162 --> 00:30:38.120
We might have noted that
a statement or a claim

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:41.510
was disputed, or that it had
proven incorrect or false.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:43.730
But we did not declare
newsmakers' statements

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:44.375
to be lies.

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:51.050
Why avoid using the L
word in news reporting?

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:54.380
Well, a lie is a
very specific thing.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:56.990
And short of a confession,
it requires a reporter

00:30:56.990 --> 00:30:59.210
to claim to know what's
inside the mind of the person

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:00.780
speaking.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.410
In fact, when somebody gives
contradictory information

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:05.630
or makes a false statement,
it could theoretically

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:07.950
be for many other reasons.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:11.750
Let's go back to the Ford and
Firestone tire rollover scandal

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:13.190
in 2000.

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:16.370
I covered a lot of stories
where the executives

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:18.830
of these corporations said
there was no evidence that there

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:19.490
was danger.

00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:23.720
They had no realization that
the tires had a safety issue.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:26.450
But that contradicted
documents that we

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:28.655
had that showed they had
discussed this long ago.

00:31:28.655 --> 00:31:31.280
It was a well documented trail.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:33.650
But I never said
they were liars.

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:36.740
It's possible, theoretically,
that their staff gave them

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.650
poor briefings, or
withheld information,

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:41.750
or they might have seen
the documents in question,

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:46.010
but misinterpreted them, or
forgotten what they said.

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:48.290
As unlikely as those
excuses might seem,

00:31:48.290 --> 00:31:50.120
it's not the place
of a news journalist

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.980
to claim to know what's
in a person's mind.

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:54.560
In fact, there are
very few instances

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:57.500
I can think of, where it's
appropriate for a reporter

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:01.887
in a news report to claim
that a newsmaker lied.

00:32:01.887 --> 00:32:03.470
There's another
important reason to be

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:06.950
circumspect as a news journalist
with accusing people of lies.

00:32:06.950 --> 00:32:09.620
To the news consumer,
such language

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:11.330
tends to sound as
if the reporter is

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:13.330
being biased or pejorative.

00:32:13.330 --> 00:32:15.510
It begins to feel very personal.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.180
It removes the
sense of neutrality

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:19.820
that we try to maintain
in our hard news reporting

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:22.620
in order to be seen as
fair reporters of fact.

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.800
It's best to stick
to the facts and let

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:28.900
members of the public form
their own conclusions.

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.260
The good practices that I've
described, the reluctance

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:33.600
to pretend to see inside
the mind of the subjects

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:35.730
we report on, used
to be considered

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.680
the norm in journalism.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:40.120
But all of that has
gone out the window.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:43.530
Now reporters frequently
take sides, boldly declaring

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:45.300
one side or one
news maker, to be

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:48.480
telling the truth, and
another, usually Trump,

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:50.550
to be lying, even
when the truth is

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.700
unproven or impossible to know,
or when the differences are

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:58.070
matters of interpretation,
or clashing opinions,

00:32:58.070 --> 00:32:59.990
or even when the
lies or exaggerations

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:02.060
are misstatements.

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:03.540
The New York Times,
as I mentioned,

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:05.600
has led the way in
declaring itself

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.430
arbiters of Trump's lies.

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:10.400
And we've seen other media
quickly followed suit.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:13.190
And biased observers
heralded it all as brave

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:15.660
and groundbreaking.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:19.460
So yesterday, I conducted a
Google search using the term

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:27.820
"Obama lies" and it
produced 41 million results.

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:30.610
I then searched the
term "Trump lies."

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:33.325
That returned 612
million results.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:39.420
In other words,
after one term, Trump

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:43.050
got about 15 times more
search results about lies

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:44.805
than Obama had after two terms.

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:55.340
A closer analysis shows that
many of Trump's internet hits

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:58.970
about lies are links to
news stories and blogs

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:01.010
and lists of his lies.

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:05.720
And in contrast, Obama's hits to
many of his articles or blogs,

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:08.840
were defending him against
accusations of lies,

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:13.820
or with Obama officials
accusing Trump of lying.

00:34:13.820 --> 00:34:15.710
Instead of providing
critical pushback

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:18.469
to this questionable
journalistic shift, in which

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:20.900
reporters call opinions of
their enemies or opinions

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:23.989
they disagree with lies,
Columbia Journalism Review

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:27.440
jumped on the bandwagon patting
The New York Times on the back

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:29.690
for calling Trump a liar.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:32.270
"A precedent has
been set," declared

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:35.119
Columbia Journalism Review
approvingly, "which is great."

00:34:40.750 --> 00:34:42.580
Columbia Journalism
Review goes on to say,

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:46.060
"By using a word that comes
from the vocabulary of advocacy

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:49.659
in its own voice, The Times,
and other news organizations,

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:52.330
have taken the truth-telling
standards of the news business

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:54.070
to a new level.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:56.170
Until the rise of
Trump," they say,

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:58.960
"only on rare occasions,
criminal convictions,

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:02.530
and instances of plagiarism
and falsifying resumes,

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:04.960
were words like 'lie'
ever used by the media

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.690
describing news events."

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:11.230
I'm taken aback to see a
Journalism Review publication

00:35:11.230 --> 00:35:14.110
explicitly applauding
a news organization

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:20.580
for using the vocabulary of
advocacy in its news reporting.

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:24.090
Author and former New York Times
reporter David Cay Johnston,

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:25.950
a Pulitzer Prize
winner, is quoted

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:29.220
as saying he was "thrilled
and flabbergasted"

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:32.730
to see the Times
call Trump a liar.

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:36.150
Why would that be a thrill
unless one is steeped in bias

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:37.680
and seeking to
advance a narrative,

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:40.330
rather than report the facts.

00:35:40.330 --> 00:35:42.130
With hearty support
from journalism groups

00:35:42.130 --> 00:35:43.630
and their peers,
it's understandable

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:45.970
that reporters would
grow even bolder.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:49.480
They began to expand
the lie label to cases

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:53.410
when targeted newsmakers are
simply exaggerating, joking,

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:54.875
or misspeaking.

00:35:54.875 --> 00:35:56.500
Or when there's no
possible way to know

00:35:56.500 --> 00:35:59.930
for sure, whether a
statement is true or false.

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:03.080
One example can be found
in news reports declaring,

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:04.760
Trump was lying
when he stated there

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:08.210
was fraud in the 2020 election,
or that the election was

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:09.650
stolen.

00:36:09.650 --> 00:36:12.620
Unless one can somehow
prove that Trump does not

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:15.620
believe the election was
stolen, but is saying it was,

00:36:15.620 --> 00:36:17.870
that wasn't a lie.

00:36:17.870 --> 00:36:19.850
Whether you like
Trump or not, this

00:36:19.850 --> 00:36:22.070
is pure journalistic
practice and further erodes

00:36:22.070 --> 00:36:25.190
our credibility as an industry.

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:32.460
Another example, Trump's 2019
State of the Union address.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:35.280
When the president
claimed, "Our brave troops

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:36.780
have now been
fighting in the Middle

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:40.560
East for almost 19 years,"
the media's eagle-eyed fact

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:42.843
checkers called that a lie.

00:36:42.843 --> 00:36:45.510
The actual length of time the US
had been fighting in the Middle

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:49.050
East, they countered,
was not almost 19 years,

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:51.870
but a bit more than 17.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:53.640
Actually the media's
own fact check

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:55.500
about the length
of the Afghan war

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:58.920
could be characterized as
a lie by its own standard.

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.850
The war began in October 2001.

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.600
The State of the Union
address in question

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:07.020
took place more
than 18 years later,

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:09.870
closer to Trump's
almost 19 years,

00:37:09.870 --> 00:37:12.550
than the media's 17 years.

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:17.130
But that doesn't fit the
Trump is a liar narrative.

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:18.920
There's a second
set of news phrases

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:23.810
the media invented and deployed,
specifically against Trump,

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:24.830
without evidence.

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:28.970
No credible evidence,
and no evidence.

00:37:28.970 --> 00:37:30.410
First, without evidence.

00:37:30.410 --> 00:37:33.170
As a news journalist, I don't
recall ever using or uttering

00:37:33.170 --> 00:37:35.270
the phrase "without
evidence" when

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:38.450
reporting on anybody or anything
over the course of about 35

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:41.510
years, until we covered Trump.

00:37:41.510 --> 00:37:43.310
At first glance,
it might seem as

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:44.960
though phrases like
"without evidence"

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:47.090
and "no credible
evidence" are more

00:37:47.090 --> 00:37:49.520
fact-based and responsible,
than tossing around

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:51.200
accusations of lies.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:53.450
But they can be
just as problematic.

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:56.570
Who decides what
evidence is credible?

00:37:56.570 --> 00:37:59.330
An absence of evidence
does not necessarily

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.550
mean a claim is discredited.

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:03.620
After all, there was
no evidence that polio

00:38:03.620 --> 00:38:07.610
could be transmitted via water
or through the oral polio

00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:10.055
vaccine, until there was.

00:38:10.055 --> 00:38:11.930
There was no evidence
that some cholesterol's

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:15.490
good for your health,
until there was.

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:17.650
Without evidence is
an invented concept

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:20.500
for the purpose of
slanted reporting.

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:22.930
Consider that throughout
time, few newsmakers

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:25.670
have presented evidence
when making statements.

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:27.820
It was never expected that
each comment or speech

00:38:27.820 --> 00:38:30.640
would be accompanied by a set
of footnotes or citations.

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:31.960
Until Trump.

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:34.120
Now, without evidence
is commonly invoked

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:36.760
in a one-sided fashion,
usually against Trump

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:38.500
and his supporters,
and typically,

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.480
when the media want to
call them into question

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:41.590
or disparage them.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:50.140
When it comes to Trump
Russia collusion,

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:52.540
and other anti-Trump
narratives, we in the media

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:56.260
allowed two years of outlandish
allegations to be made,

00:38:56.260 --> 00:39:00.730
virtually free of challenge
and without credible evidence.

00:39:00.730 --> 00:39:03.880
Many of those claims
proved to be untrue.

00:39:03.880 --> 00:39:05.350
And there were more.

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:08.260
Trump may have not paid any
income taxes for decades,

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:09.550
if ever.

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:12.010
He never really wanted
to be president.

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:14.590
He's lying about
immigration being a problem.

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:17.410
He was Russian President
Vladimir Putin's stooge, a spy

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:20.230
for Russia, the list goes on.

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:22.870
At the same time we're
calling out Trump,

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:25.030
we're violating the very
evidentiary standards

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:27.130
to which we hold him.

00:39:27.130 --> 00:39:29.080
In our zeal to get
Trump, we are letting

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:30.940
our own journalistic
principles slide.

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:34.930
We've laid bare our own bias
and our own double standards.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:37.510
We can blame Trump all we
like for the death of the news

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:38.840
as we once knew it.

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:40.930
But the truth is, we've
done it to ourselves.

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:45.730
If you're interested,
my book Slanted

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.130
goes in far more depth
about all of this.

00:39:48.130 --> 00:39:51.160
I interviewed a lot of
colleagues and top network news

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:52.810
executives.

00:39:52.810 --> 00:39:55.990
And there's an appendix that
lists the media mistakes

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:58.480
that I categorize one by one.

00:39:58.480 --> 00:40:01.120
The mistakes made against Trump.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:04.000
When reporters get caught
doing bad journalism,

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:06.820
like I've described today,
they and their colleagues

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:10.900
often retort that Trump
lied more than we do.

00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:14.740
Or Trump mocks the media
and his enemies on Twitter,

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:16.480
before he was banned.

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:20.050
The fact is, Trump mercilessly
mocks, and is mercilessly

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:22.870
mocked by, his
political detractors,

00:40:22.870 --> 00:40:24.730
such as leading
congressional Democrats

00:40:24.730 --> 00:40:27.880
Nancy Pelosi, Chuck
Schumer, Adam Schiff.

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:30.280
Collectively, political
figures have no obligation

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:31.580
to be neutral or fair.

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:35.410
In fact, the nature of politics
breeds this kind of dynamic.

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:37.420
But journalists are different.

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:41.140
Our professional obligation is
to cover our subjects fairly.

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:43.515
We must maintain the
same high standard, even

00:40:43.515 --> 00:40:44.890
when we don't like
somebody we're

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:49.150
covering, especially perhaps,
when we don't like that person.

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:51.940
Otherwise, why have
standards at all?

00:40:51.940 --> 00:40:55.150
The implication that somehow
the media's mistakes and attacks

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:57.670
are justified because
of what we believe

00:40:57.670 --> 00:41:00.580
to be the flawed moral
character of our subject,

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:02.260
is a precarious one.

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:04.120
The way we cover
those we perceive

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:06.370
as the enemies of
our own viewpoints

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:10.810
defines how well we do
our job covering the news.

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:13.420
And I'm happy to
take some questions.

00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:15.895
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:26.300 --> 00:41:28.360
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you, Miss Attkisson.

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:30.970
If you have a question,
please raise your hand

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:33.390
and wait for a microphone
to be brought to you.

00:41:37.390 --> 00:41:39.050
AUDIENCE: Could
you please comment

00:41:39.050 --> 00:41:43.025
on the influence of the Poynter
Institute in Journalism?

00:41:43.025 --> 00:41:44.900
SHARYL ATTKISSON: Yes,
the Poynter Institute.

00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:48.050
I've discussed some of
that in my book Slanted.

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:50.390
Like the Columbia
Journalism Review,

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:53.960
and a lot of other non-profits
or journalism organizations,

00:41:53.960 --> 00:41:55.790
they've all been
co-opted to some degree

00:41:55.790 --> 00:41:59.270
by the same political
or corporate interests,

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:02.840
accepting funding from the same
handful of dubious sources,

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:05.990
in my view, partisan
sources that fund the fact

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:09.620
checks, whether it's on social
media or news organizations

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:11.390
or these non-profits.

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:12.680
It's the same group of people.

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:15.440
And you can see the
change in their reporting.

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:20.060
Maybe, now, Poynter Institute
relies on Media Matters,

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:23.960
this is one example, as
a source in its writing,

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:25.640
without disclosing
that Media Matters is

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:27.290
a partisan propaganda group.

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:29.750
And so young people are
learning this from them

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:33.440
because they're told to look to
Poynter Institute as a standard

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:34.970
in journalism and ethics.

00:42:34.970 --> 00:42:38.170
So there's a problem from
virtually every angle

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:39.920
we're looking when it
comes to journalism.

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:43.130
The organizations that
are supposed to help

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.530
watch over us, or set
standards for the kind

00:42:45.530 --> 00:42:48.440
of reporting we do, they're
cheering on this trend

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:49.550
that I'm talking about.

00:42:53.882 --> 00:42:56.090
SPEAKER 2: There's a question
to the speaker's right.

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:56.930
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:00.770
What do the people who are
writing the slanted news

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:01.745
hope to gain?

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:06.150
SHARYL ATTKISSON: It's a mix.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:07.470
It's a complicated question.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:12.300
Because in some instances,
there are more traditional news

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:15.030
journalists who are censoring
themselves and shaping

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:17.490
what they report because
they're rewarded for it.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:19.860
They're moving up in the
hierarchy of the newsroom

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:22.470
when they embrace this
sort of reporting.

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:24.790
And they're not rewarded
when they're not doing so.

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:25.290
So.

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:27.990
There's an element of
people transforming

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:31.548
themselves to keep their job
and advance their careers

00:43:31.548 --> 00:43:33.090
But I think one of
the bigger factors

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:36.480
is the trend I described,
where propagandists,

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:38.130
political and
corporate interests,

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:40.800
have infiltrated the news,
not just figuring out

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:45.930
how to influence what we report,
and lobbyists and send us,

00:43:45.930 --> 00:43:49.950
by way of third
parties and nonprofits,

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:52.470
talking points and things
that are supposed to be news

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:53.550
that we then pick up.

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:55.830
They now work for
us in our newsrooms.

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:58.650
We hire them as
editorial people.

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:02.610
We bring them on as analysts and
pay them six figure salaries.

00:44:02.610 --> 00:44:04.650
They should be paying
us to appear on our air

00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:06.150
and distribute their
talking points.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:09.340
But we're paying them
handsomely to do so.

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:13.290
So I think the news business
has been transformed, where

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:16.170
now there's no
firewall between us

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:20.310
and the interest trying to
slant the news because they have

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:21.660
a whole different goal in mind.

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:23.320
We're one and the same.

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:25.320
And that explains, and
really is only thing that

00:44:25.320 --> 00:44:28.620
explains, why so many
formerly well-respected news

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:31.740
organizations are
getting so much wrong,

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:34.215
and yet there's very
little repercussion

00:44:34.215 --> 00:44:35.340
for that kind of reporting.

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:38.220
The people who do
those stories continue

00:44:38.220 --> 00:44:41.190
to be reported and heralded
and given awards and so on,

00:44:41.190 --> 00:44:43.590
because we now are them.

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:45.570
We're one and the same
as the propagandists,

00:44:45.570 --> 00:44:46.590
in many instances.

00:44:49.490 --> 00:44:52.760
SPEAKER 3: We have a question
on the speaker's left.

00:44:52.760 --> 00:44:55.370
AUDIENCE: Ma'am, would
you please comment

00:44:55.370 --> 00:44:58.580
on the massive collusion between
the mainstream media, the,

00:44:58.580 --> 00:45:02.030
social and a number
of government agencies

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:06.230
to totally censor the Hunter
Biden corruption scandal that

00:45:06.230 --> 00:45:08.960
was first broken by
the New York Post,

00:45:08.960 --> 00:45:11.330
and what is possible
implications

00:45:11.330 --> 00:45:14.098
were for the election outcome?

00:45:14.098 --> 00:45:16.640
SHARYL ATTKISSON: Well, I can't
say what would have happened.

00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:19.520
Although, there's been some
polling done talking about what

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:21.870
could have influenced
the election

00:45:21.870 --> 00:45:24.072
if this news had gotten out.

00:45:24.072 --> 00:45:25.280
And I'll point out one thing.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:29.420
The New York Post didn't
really break that story.

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:32.060
The Hunter Biden story
was well fleshed out

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:37.061
and discussed by liberal
news outlets the fall before.

00:45:37.061 --> 00:45:41.570
Before Biden was the Democrats'
nominee, there were, I take it,

00:45:41.570 --> 00:45:44.000
other Democrats that
were planting stories

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:47.060
in the press to try to keep
Biden from being the nominee.

00:45:47.060 --> 00:45:50.280
And so some in the left-leaning
press did some very good

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:53.810
and important exposés on the
Hunter Biden alleged conflicts

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:57.110
of interest, as well as other
members of the Biden family.

00:45:57.110 --> 00:45:58.700
But once Biden
became the nominee,

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:01.820
fast forward to this
story sort of resurfacing

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:06.443
with news of his computer
being confiscated by the FBI.

00:46:06.443 --> 00:46:07.943
And all of a sudden,
everybody acted

00:46:07.943 --> 00:46:09.740
like it was the first
they'd heard of it.

00:46:09.740 --> 00:46:12.560
And then many on the left
acted like it was a conspiracy

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:14.540
theory, even though
they had well reported

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:16.790
on this some months before.

00:46:16.790 --> 00:46:19.550
And it's just the
craziest dynamic.

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:21.780
Glenn Greenwald
of The Intercept,

00:46:21.780 --> 00:46:23.300
who is a left-leaning
reporter who

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:27.650
thinks much like I do
about news and news trends,

00:46:27.650 --> 00:46:30.223
he started The
Intercept with the goal

00:46:30.223 --> 00:46:32.390
in mind of having a news
organization that would not

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:35.150
be beholden to political
and corporate interests

00:46:35.150 --> 00:46:38.150
in the same way as what
we call legacy media.

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:40.880
And you may know that Glenn
Greenwald quit his own news

00:46:40.880 --> 00:46:43.010
organization because
they censored

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:44.870
his story on Hunter Biden.

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:49.520
This is not just a problem
of the left versus right.

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:51.050
There's just a
problem, in general,

00:46:51.050 --> 00:46:52.800
by those seeking
to manage the news.

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:55.400
It's not always
along partisan lines.

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:57.770
Again, I think we're left
to guess what impact it

00:46:57.770 --> 00:47:00.700
might have had on the election.

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:04.100
SPEAKER 2: We have a question
on the speaker's right.

00:47:04.100 --> 00:47:07.880
AUDIENCE: Do the news reporters,
particularly the TV reporters,

00:47:07.880 --> 00:47:10.940
actually believe what
they're reporting,

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:13.460
or are they simply
following what they're

00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:15.830
told to do from higher ups?

00:47:15.830 --> 00:47:18.950
And then secondly, how do
corporate interests, which

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:21.620
you mentioned, gain
from false news?

00:47:24.955 --> 00:47:27.080
SHARYL ATTKISSON: If I
forget your second question,

00:47:27.080 --> 00:47:27.997
remind me in a minute.

00:47:27.997 --> 00:47:30.800
But I get asked all the time
where the reporters believe

00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:33.080
what they're talking
about, especially when it's

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:37.100
proven later to be wildly
wrong or when it's very biased.

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:40.880
And again, I think
that's a mixed answer.

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:43.370
I know some of the reporters
involved that you would think

00:47:43.370 --> 00:47:47.690
are very biased, who used to be
fairly straight-news reporters.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:49.640
But they've gone to
a news organization

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:53.540
where there's a powerful
dynamic of groupthink and peer

00:47:53.540 --> 00:47:56.485
pressure, where the bosses want
a certain kind of reporting.

00:47:56.485 --> 00:47:57.860
And when you
deliver that, you're

00:47:57.860 --> 00:47:59.720
surrounded by people
who pat you on the back

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:01.400
and tell you how
brilliant you are.

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:04.760
And maybe you were
not considered really

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:06.830
too far above average
in the old dynamic,

00:48:06.830 --> 00:48:09.200
but now you're a big star
because of all these kinds

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:11.050
of things you're reporting.

00:48:11.050 --> 00:48:13.010
So I don't know if
they become a believer

00:48:13.010 --> 00:48:16.553
in giving their own opinion and
thinking that it's valuable,

00:48:16.553 --> 00:48:18.470
and even when proven
wrong, if they have a way

00:48:18.470 --> 00:48:21.620
to sort of justify it because
they don't get punished for it,

00:48:21.620 --> 00:48:23.930
and they're still being
rewarded by their bosses.

00:48:23.930 --> 00:48:28.310
But then, the second dynamic is,
and I believe strongly in this,

00:48:28.310 --> 00:48:30.320
as I said, we've hired
these propagandists.

00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:32.810
They've found ways to be
hired in our newsrooms.

00:48:32.810 --> 00:48:34.640
So they don't think
it's a mistake.

00:48:34.640 --> 00:48:36.900
And even if they
know they're wrong,

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:38.960
and even if they get
caught after the fact

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:41.450
having disseminated
false information,

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.060
they're not embarrassed
because they still accomplished

00:48:44.060 --> 00:48:44.940
their goal.

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:46.398
If they're not
journalists, they're

00:48:46.398 --> 00:48:48.680
not trying to tell the
truth, necessarily.

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:50.990
They're trying to put
forth a narrative.

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:53.480
And if they succeeded at that,
even if the narrative isn't

00:48:53.480 --> 00:48:56.090
true, they've still
done their job the way

00:48:56.090 --> 00:48:58.490
they hope to do it in my view.

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:07.450
AUDIENCE: Does this not prove
President Trump's comment,

00:49:07.450 --> 00:49:11.020
not so much fake news, but
the news media has become

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:13.548
truly enemy of the people?

00:49:13.548 --> 00:49:15.590
SHARYL ATTKISSON: Well,
that's for you to decide.

00:49:15.590 --> 00:49:18.860
But I do think he made
a lot of good points.

00:49:18.860 --> 00:49:24.130
And as I talked about in
my last book, most of you

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:27.670
may think President Trump
thought up the phrase "fake

00:49:27.670 --> 00:49:30.070
news" in its modern context.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:31.750
That was a liberal invention.

00:49:31.750 --> 00:49:35.800
And I traced that to
September of 2016.

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:38.530
It was first used in
its modern context

00:49:38.530 --> 00:49:41.860
by a nonprofit
called "First Draft."

00:49:41.860 --> 00:49:44.260
And shortly after that,
President Obama gave a speech

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:46.240
at Carnegie Mellon,
in which he suggested,

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:48.970
for the first time I ever
heard that somebody needed

00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:51.340
to step in and curate
our information,

00:49:51.340 --> 00:49:54.580
start curating the internet
in this Wild, Wild West media

00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:56.260
environment, as he called it.

00:49:56.260 --> 00:49:59.872
And I remember thinking at the
time, if you can take yourself

00:49:59.872 --> 00:50:01.330
back just a couple
of years, nobody

00:50:01.330 --> 00:50:04.090
was asking for people,
third parties, to come in

00:50:04.090 --> 00:50:06.195
and censor and curate our news.

00:50:06.195 --> 00:50:07.570
They had to create
the appearance

00:50:07.570 --> 00:50:10.120
of a demand for such
a program so that they

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:11.440
could control the news.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:15.280
So I went back seeing that
there was some kind of operation

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:16.330
going on, here.

00:50:16.330 --> 00:50:18.070
Who is First Draft?

00:50:18.070 --> 00:50:19.720
And I called this
nonprofit and said,

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:20.970
where do you get your funding?

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:23.290
Because they hadn't
filed tax forms yet.

00:50:23.290 --> 00:50:25.948
And they admitted that they
got their funding from Google.

00:50:25.948 --> 00:50:28.240
And they were invented around
the start of the election

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:31.330
cycle, this non-profit, that
invented the concept of going

00:50:31.330 --> 00:50:35.350
after fake news, which was just
defined at the time as mostly

00:50:35.350 --> 00:50:37.270
conservative news.

00:50:37.270 --> 00:50:41.230
And Google, is
owned by Alphabet,

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:43.810
who was led at the
time by a man named

00:50:43.810 --> 00:50:45.730
Eric Schmidt, who was
a top Hillary Clinton

00:50:45.730 --> 00:50:47.230
supporter and donor.

00:50:47.230 --> 00:50:49.982
So I think that was
a big operation,

00:50:49.982 --> 00:50:51.190
the beginning of the efforts.

00:50:51.190 --> 00:50:53.650
They saw, Trump's
enemies, that he

00:50:53.650 --> 00:50:55.150
was making inroads
with the public,

00:50:55.150 --> 00:50:57.000
despite their
control of the news.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:50:59.500
And they saw that they had to
do, or thought they had to do,

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:00.708
something about the internet.

00:51:00.708 --> 00:51:04.090
Because they blamed the
internet for the rise of Trump,

00:51:04.090 --> 00:51:08.290
outside of this controlled
news information landscape.

00:51:08.290 --> 00:51:10.180
Trump took over the
fake news phrase,

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:11.980
being the master
marketer that he is.

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:14.200
I call it a hostile takeover.

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:16.210
Every time they cried
fake news, he just

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:17.500
like threw it back at them.

00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:18.940
You're fake news.

00:51:18.940 --> 00:51:21.250
And pretty soon he
was so good at it,

00:51:21.250 --> 00:51:24.520
that by January following the
September when this started,

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:26.440
The Washington Post is
printing articles that

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:28.450
were saying things
like, it's time

00:51:28.450 --> 00:51:31.360
to retire the term fake news.

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:34.510
They didn't like how it's
being used now by Trump.

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:39.790
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:40.667
There seems to be--

00:51:40.667 --> 00:51:42.500
SHARYL ATTKISSON: I
can't see where you are.

00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:43.000
I'm sorry.

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:44.410
AUDIENCE: I'm right over here.

00:51:44.410 --> 00:51:46.207
SPEAKER 3: We have a
question in the back.

00:51:46.207 --> 00:51:47.540
To the back left of the speaker.

00:51:47.540 --> 00:51:47.900
SHARYL ATTKISSON: OK.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:48.508
OK.

00:51:48.508 --> 00:51:49.300
AUDIENCE: Hi there.

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:51.110
Thank you for being here.

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:55.850
There seems to be a
virus among our media,

00:51:55.850 --> 00:51:59.230
particularly even in
conservative media, lately,

00:51:59.230 --> 00:52:03.790
to even question the
validity of the election.

00:52:03.790 --> 00:52:06.610
I'm noticing it on
talk radio and it's

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:10.120
almost as if they will
not even bring it up.

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:12.430
And they deflect it
when anyone calls in.

00:52:12.430 --> 00:52:16.060
And I'm assuming we know
what happens, right?

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:19.930
And I'm just wondering,
is it a matter of, maybe,

00:52:19.930 --> 00:52:27.100
buying time until we have some
more secure platforms to be

00:52:27.100 --> 00:52:28.420
more honest?

00:52:28.420 --> 00:52:30.970
Is this, perhaps,
what's going on?

00:52:30.970 --> 00:52:36.940
Or are we relegated to just
this very limited narrative

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:39.437
for fear of being canceled.

00:52:39.437 --> 00:52:41.020
SHARYL ATTKISSON:
It's a good question

00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:46.360
and I think we should all be
cognizant of not being bullied

00:52:46.360 --> 00:52:50.200
out of using our common sense,
making our own solutions,

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:52.660
seeking out answers and
various sides to stories,

00:52:52.660 --> 00:52:54.280
and saying what we think.

00:52:54.280 --> 00:52:57.730
My own view is, only that which
is illegal, should be censored,

00:52:57.730 --> 00:52:58.923
and the rest is fair game.

00:52:58.923 --> 00:53:01.090
It's up to us to figure out
what we want to believe.

00:53:01.090 --> 00:53:03.340
And by the way, if we want to
believe something that's false,

00:53:03.340 --> 00:53:05.400
we have a right to do
that in this country.

00:53:05.400 --> 00:53:07.150
It's not up to somebody
else to say, well,

00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:09.970
you shouldn't be
thinking such things,

00:53:09.970 --> 00:53:12.370
or talking about such things.

00:53:12.370 --> 00:53:14.350
But being an optimist,
and I've talked

00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:17.020
to some people that are
looking for solutions,

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.180
I think there will be
something that fleshes out

00:53:19.180 --> 00:53:20.230
in the next four years.

00:53:20.230 --> 00:53:24.940
Because, as I like to say, the
truth finds a way to be told.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:27.217
And over history,
and over time, when

00:53:27.217 --> 00:53:29.050
people have been
suppressed, and their views

00:53:29.050 --> 00:53:30.550
have been suppressed,
they've always

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:32.890
found a way, at least
in this country,

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:35.050
to express how they feel.

00:53:35.050 --> 00:53:37.060
And there are people
working on the challenges

00:53:37.060 --> 00:53:38.890
from three standpoints.

00:53:38.890 --> 00:53:41.980
There are investors
who do want to invest

00:53:41.980 --> 00:53:45.610
in fair reporting
and open information.

00:53:45.610 --> 00:53:47.020
So the money is there.

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:48.868
They just don't know
where to put it.

00:53:48.868 --> 00:53:50.410
But number two,
there are people that

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:54.430
are trying to
accomplish the question

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:58.030
or the task of how do
you not get deplatformed.

00:53:58.030 --> 00:54:01.930
And they're trying to invent
ways outside Big Tech.

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:03.250
We're addicts.

00:54:03.250 --> 00:54:05.290
We got hooked on Big
Tech when they weren't

00:54:05.290 --> 00:54:06.580
trying to do this censorship.

00:54:06.580 --> 00:54:08.060
We thought it was wonderful.

00:54:08.060 --> 00:54:10.400
And now we all
communicate that way.

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:12.910
And they've sort of done
this bait and switch.

00:54:12.910 --> 00:54:15.880
Now they can control us because
we're all relying on them.

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:17.870
And they're changing the rules.

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:21.130
Well, there are ways involving,
far beyond my understanding,

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:24.220
but involving things like
blockchain technology.

00:54:24.220 --> 00:54:25.900
There are ways to
design platforms

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:29.390
that can't be so controlled
by what we consider Big Tech

00:54:29.390 --> 00:54:29.890
media.

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:32.110
There are people working
on that problem, too.

00:54:32.110 --> 00:54:35.140
And then there are
news people, like me,

00:54:35.140 --> 00:54:39.400
who want to report
in a open way showing

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:41.890
different viewpoints and
different scientific studies

00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:42.970
and different opinions.

00:54:42.970 --> 00:54:46.450
So those three things, I think,
will come together, somehow

00:54:46.450 --> 00:54:48.460
in the next four years or so.

00:54:48.460 --> 00:54:51.620
And there will be new emerging
technologies and solutions.

00:54:51.620 --> 00:54:54.670
But I would say in
the meantime, we

00:54:54.670 --> 00:54:56.920
have to work really hard
not to be bullied out

00:54:56.920 --> 00:54:59.350
of being Americans.

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:04.600
And being heard, whatever we
think, and whatever we feel.

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:06.820
Particularly when you're
talking about the election,

00:55:06.820 --> 00:55:10.330
to criminalize
feelings and thoughts,

00:55:10.330 --> 00:55:13.480
about something as
important as the election,

00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:15.040
even when you're
using common sense

00:55:15.040 --> 00:55:17.590
and calling into question
some things that happened.

00:55:17.590 --> 00:55:19.180
Then to be treated
like, or told,

00:55:19.180 --> 00:55:21.760
that you're practically a
criminal for thinking that,

00:55:21.760 --> 00:55:22.840
or for expressing that.

00:55:22.840 --> 00:55:25.030
This is a very dangerous thing.

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:26.654
And I hope we don't allow it.

00:55:26.654 --> 00:55:28.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:34.400 --> 00:55:38.318
SPEAKER 3: We have time
for one more question.

00:55:38.318 --> 00:55:39.860
AUDIENCE: It's really
not a question,

00:55:39.860 --> 00:55:42.050
it's more of an acknowledgment.

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:44.420
As soon as your
first book came out.

00:55:44.420 --> 00:55:46.055
I read it and loved it.

00:55:46.055 --> 00:55:48.050
And when you announced
the second book,

00:55:48.050 --> 00:55:49.460
I got an early copy.

00:55:49.460 --> 00:55:51.620
And as soon as I
got it, I read it.

00:55:51.620 --> 00:55:53.390
And I'm a big fan.

00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:55.280
I just want you to
know that you're

00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:58.340
a person who is
intellectually honest

00:55:58.340 --> 00:56:00.830
and has the courage
to tell the truth,

00:56:00.830 --> 00:56:05.450
despite all the crap that
you're getting from the media.

00:56:05.450 --> 00:56:07.250
I want to acknowledge
you for being here.

00:56:07.250 --> 00:56:10.860
You're one of the reasons I came
here today and this weekend.

00:56:10.860 --> 00:56:11.360
Thank you.

00:56:11.360 --> 00:56:12.527
SHARYL ATTKISSON: Thank you.

00:56:12.527 --> 00:56:14.120
Thank you very much.

00:56:14.120 --> 00:56:15.030
Thanks, everybody.

00:56:15.030 --> 00:56:17.780
[APPLAUSE]

